  "raw_label": "appellant_lost",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "G.Kannan vs The Designated Officer on 28 November, 2024 Author: G.K.Ilanthiraiyan Bench: G.K.Ilanthiraiyan W.P.( Before The Madurai Bench Of Madras High Court Dated : 28.11.2024 Coram: The Honourable Mr. Justice G.K.Ilanthiraiyan W.P.(MD)No.21172 of 2018 and W.M.P(MD)No.19014 of 2018 G.Kannan ...Petitioner -Vs- 1.The Designated Officer, Food Safety and Drug Administration Department, Dindigul 624 001 2.The Director, FSSAI Referral Laboratory, Food Testing Laboratory, Indian Institute of Food Processing Technology, Thanjavur 600 018. ...Respondents Prayer : Writ Petition filed under Article 226 of Constitution of India for the issuance of a Writ of Certiorari, calling for the records pertai impugned Analysis Report of the 2nd respondent dated 16/07/2018 in Certificate No.F-040/18-19 and quash the same as illegal. For Petitioner : Mr.K.Krishna For Respondents : Mr.D.Gandhi Raj Special Government Plead for RR1 & 2 https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis Page 1 of 6 W.P.(MD G.Kannan vs The Designated Officer on 28 November, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/19132855/ 1 ORDER",
    "facts": "2.The petitioner is the manufacturer of the drinking water. While being so, the samples of water bottles were collected by the first respondent on 18.08.2017. Subsequently, Food Analyst has submitted a report dated 23.11.2017 stating that out of eleven parameters, two parameters exceeded the permissible limit under regulation 2.10.8 of Food Safety and Standards Regulations, 2011. Therefore, The samples were declared as unsafe. Thereafter, an appeal was filed before the second respondent by sending another samples for analysis as contemplated under section Act 2.4.6(1) of the Food and Standards Rules, 2011. The report was sent by the second respondent on 16.07.2018. The water bottles were manufactured on 16.08.2017 and the analysis was conducted after a period of six months. The Section 46(3) of the Food Safety and https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis Standards Act, 2006 provides only fourteen days to submit the analysis report from the date of receipt of the samples whereas the food analysis has submitted a report only on 23.11.2017. However, it was sent to the food analysis. The Food analysis report was sent within a period of 14 days as contemplated under Section 46 (3) of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 and as such, he made a request to the first respondent seeking further time to submit a report by its communication dated 23.11.2017 due to vacancies of Senior Analyst and other staffs. Therefore, there is absolutely no violation to analyse the water bottles by the food analyst as well as the second respondent. The procedures were followed in accordance with law and submitted a report and on the strength of the report, the first respondent has accorded sanction for classification and accordingly, the petitioner is facing prosecution in S.T.C.No.1848 of 2021 on the file of the Judicial Magistrate No.III, Dindigul.",
    "arguments": "4.The learned Counsel appearing for the writ petitioner has relied upon the judgment of this Court in Crl.O.P(MD)No.18098 of 2018, dated 05.01.2012. 5.The respondent has filed counter and the submission made by the learned Special Government Pleader appearing for the respondents reveals that the samples of water bottles were taken from the petitioner on 18.08.2017."
  },
